    "a1/clothes-and-shopping"
  ],
  "assessments": [
    "a0-exit"
  ],
  "tutorReviewRequired": true,
  "qualityReview": {
    "status": "editorial-review",
    "generatedPedagogy": true,
    "learnerPilotCount": 0,
    "scoredItemCount": 45,
    "revisionSummary": "Adjective meaning and position, be agreement, article choice, adjective order, color accessibility, relative meanings, U.S. usage, pronunciation, item context, feedback, production, and retrieval were audited; three learner pilots remain."
  }
});
---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 12</p>
  <h1>Colors &amp; basic <em>adjectives</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Identify and describe everyday things with a color and a useful quality: <span class="ex">a small black notebook</span> or <span class="ex">The notebook is small and black.</span> Place the adjective correctly, keep it unchanged with plural nouns, and add another identifier when color alone is not enough.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">colors and qualities</span>
    <span class="tag">two adjective positions</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional practice</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Adjective before the noun.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Two places: before the noun, after be.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Match opposites, choose, and build.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Describe your phone, car, and city.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">A descriptive adjective does not change for a plural noun. Say <span class="ex">one red car</span> and <span class="ex">two red cars</span>. The noun changes to <span class="ex">cars</span>; the adjective stays <span class="ex">red</span>.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved an before a vowel sound, plural -s on the noun, and are with the plural subject these chairs.">
    <h3>Bring articles, plurals, and <em>be</em> into a description</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use earlier lessons. Complete each full description, then say it aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier grammar choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a">a</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an">an</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="car">car</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="cars">cars</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is">is</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are">are</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>This is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an" data-hint="Old begins with a vowel sound, and the article comes immediately before old." data-fix="This is an old jacket." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> old jacket. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(articles)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>I can see two red <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="cars" data-hint="Two requires a plural noun, so add -s to car, not red." data-fix="I can see two red cars." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(plurals)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>These chairs <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are" data-hint="These chairs is a plural noun phrase, so use the plural form of be." data-fix="These chairs are new." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> new. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(be agreement)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Two pairs: one phrase in each pair is right</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">a red car &nbsp; · &nbsp; a car red</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="ex">The phone is new. &nbsp; · &nbsp; The phone new.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>In <strong>a red car</strong>, where does the color word go: <strong>before</strong> or <strong>after</strong> the thing (the car)?</li>
      <li>In <strong>The phone is new</strong>, the adjective comes after one short word. Which word is it?</li>
      <li>For the adjectives in this lesson, there are two useful positions: right <strong>before a noun</strong>, or right <strong>after <em>be</em></strong>. Which two phrases above need repair?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. The contexts identify the conventional color words green, blue, white, red, and black.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose the color the cue describes</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">The hint in brackets describes the color of a real object</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="red">red</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="blue">blue</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="green">green</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="white">white</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="black">black</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Healthy spring grass in bright daylight usually looks <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="green" data-hint="Choose the conventional color word for healthy grass." data-fix="Healthy spring grass usually looks green." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(healthy grass)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A cloudless daytime sky usually looks <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="blue" data-hint="Choose the conventional color word for a clear daytime sky." data-fix="A cloudless daytime sky usually looks blue." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(clear daytime sky)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Fresh, clean snow in daylight appears <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="white" data-hint="Choose the conventional color word for clean snow." data-fix="Fresh, clean snow appears white." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(clean snow)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>A standard U.S. STOP sign is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="red" data-hint="Choose the required background color of a standard U.S. STOP sign." data-fix="A standard U.S. STOP sign is red." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(U.S. road sign)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Coffee with no milk is called <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="black" data-hint="English uses this conventional color word for coffee without milk." data-fix="Coffee without milk is called black coffee." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> coffee. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(the usual phrase)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The words: colors and opposites</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>These panels pair a representative color with its written name. Screens, lighting, and people can show or perceive a shade differently, so learn the word and not only the swatch.</p>
  <div class="swatch-grid" aria-label="Color swatches">
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#E03131" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">red</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#1971C2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">blue</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#2F9E44" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">green</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#F2C200" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">yellow</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#111111" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">black</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#FFFFFF" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">white</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#868E96" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">gray</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#8B5E34" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">brown</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#F76707" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">orange</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#F06595" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">pink</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#7048E8" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">purple</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#4DABF7" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">light blue</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#1864AB" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">dark blue</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#15AABF" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">turquoise</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#C9A227" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">gold</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#CED4DA" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">silver</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#D8C9A3" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">beige</span></div>
    <div class="swatch"><span class="sw" style="background:#842029" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="nm">maroon</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Color helps identify; it should not be the only clue</h3>
    <p>For directions, safety, or finding an object, add another visible or spoken identifier. Say <span class="ex">the red button on the right</span>, <span class="ex">the small blue bag</span>, or <span class="ex">the green folder labeled “Invoices.”</span> This helps when a shade looks different on another screen or when a person does not distinguish the same colors.</p>
    <p>This course uses the U.S. spelling <span class="ex">gray</span>. In other varieties of English, you may also see the alternative spelling with <span class="ex">e</span> before <span class="ex">y</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="lead-rule" style="margin-top:2.4rem"></div>
  <p>Now add seven common quality words. Each one has an opposite:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>adjective</th><th>opposite</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>big</b></td><td><b>small</b></td><td><span class="contraction">a big office · a small phone</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>new</b></td><td><b>old</b></td><td><span class="contraction">a new car · an old jacket</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>hot</b></td><td><b>cold</b></td><td><span class="contraction">hot coffee · cold water</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>good</b></td><td><b>bad</b></td><td><span class="contraction">a good idea · a bad day</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>long</b></td><td><b>short</b></td><td><span class="contraction">a long street · a short walk</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>cheap</b></td><td><b>expensive</b></td><td><span class="contraction">a cheap bag · an expensive watch</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>fast</b></td><td><b>slow</b></td><td><span class="contraction">a fast train · a slow bus</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Some quality words need a comparison</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Big, small, fast, slow, cheap</span>, and <span class="ex">expensive</span> depend on the situation. A $900 watch is expensive for a buyer with a $100 budget, but the same price may not be expensive in every comparison.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Cheap</span> means low in price, but it can also suggest low quality. <span class="ex">Inexpensive</span> is the more neutral choice when you mean only “not expensive.”</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: find the strong part of the word</h3>
    <p>Short colors such as <span class="ex">red</span>, <span class="ex">blue</span>, and <span class="ex">green</span> have one beat. Longer words have one part that sounds stronger.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>say it in parts</th><th>what to notice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>orange</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>OR</strong>-ange</span></td><td>two spoken parts; the first is strong</td></tr>
        <tr><td>purple</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>PUR</strong>-ple</span></td><td>two spoken parts; the second is light</td></tr>
        <tr><td>turquoise</td><td><span class="ex"><strong>TUR</strong>-quoise</span></td><td>the last part sounds like “koyz”</td></tr>
        <tr><td>beige</td><td><span class="ex">“bayzh”</span></td><td>one syllable; it has the vowel in day and the soft final sound in vision</td></tr>
        <tr><td>expensive</td><td><span class="ex">ex-<strong>PEN</strong>-sive</span></td><td>the middle part is strong</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>In a neutral adjective-and-noun chunk, keep the words together and the noun often carries the clearest beat: <span class="ex">a red <strong>CAR</strong> · a purple <strong>BAG</strong> · an expensive <strong>WATCH</strong></span>. Contrast can move the beat: <span class="ex">a <strong>RED</strong> car, not a blue one.</span></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Four patterns to keep</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Before the noun:</strong> the adjective goes first. <span class="ex">a red car</span>, <span class="ex">a big office</span>, <span class="ex">a new phone</span>, not <span class="ex">a car red</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>After <em>be</em>:</strong> choose <em>be</em> for the subject, then keep the adjective unchanged: <span class="ex">The car is red. The cars are red.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Article before the whole description:</strong> choose <span class="ex">a</span> or <span class="ex">an</span> from the next sound: <span class="ex">a new watch · an old watch · an expensive watch.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Two adjectives:</strong> before a noun, use <span class="ex">a small black notebook</span>. After <em>be</em>, connect both qualities with <span class="ex">and</span>: <span class="ex">The notebook is small and black.</span></li>
      <li><strong>No plural ending on the adjective:</strong> <span class="ex">two red cars</span>, not <span class="ex">two reds cars</span>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>English has a larger adjective-order system. For this A0 lesson, practice <strong>size, age, or opinion + color + noun</strong>: <span class="ex">a small black notebook · a new blue phone · an expensive silver watch.</span></p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read color and adjective listening check">
    <h2>Hear the description</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Keep the script closed while you listen. Your teacher reads each line once, then once more. Choose the exact adjective or color phrase you hear.</p>

    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished six useful color, size, temperature, price, and shade descriptions in complete noun phrases.">
      <h3>Listening check: complete the description</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Listening adjective choices">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="green">green</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="gray">gray</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="black">black</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="blue">blue</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="small">small</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="big">big</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="cold">cold</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hot">hot</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="expensive">expensive</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="inexpensive">inexpensive</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="dark blue">dark blue</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="light blue">light blue</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>She has a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="green" data-hint="Listen for the final /n/ in green; gray has no final /n/." data-fix="She has a green bag." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> bag.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>It is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="black" data-hint="Listen for black before phone, not blue." data-fix="It is a black phone." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> phone.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>We work in a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="small" data-hint="Listen for small, the opposite of big." data-fix="We work in a small office." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> office.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>He wants <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="cold" data-hint="Listen for cold, the opposite of hot." data-fix="He wants cold water." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> water.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>That is an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="expensive" data-hint="Listen for ex-PEN-sive beginning directly with ex; inexpensive has an extra in- at the beginning." data-fix="That is an expensive watch." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> watch.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>I need the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="dark blue" data-hint="Listen for dark before blue, not light." data-fix="I need the dark blue shirt." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> shirt.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions">
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
      </div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>

    <details class="note">
      <summary>Tutor script · open only after the learner answers</summary>
      <p>Read naturally: <span class="ex">1. She has a green bag. 2. It is a black phone. 3. We work in a small office. 4. He wants cold water. 5. That is an expensive watch. 6. I need the dark blue shirt.</span></p>
      <p>For a contrast round, replace each answer with the paired distractor and ask the learner to identify the changed word.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <h2>Match the opposites</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>

  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-game data-success-message="Correct. Small, old, cold, and expensive complete the four opposite pairs used in this lesson.">
    <h3>Drill 1: match each word to its opposite</h3>
    <p class="tile-target">Tap a word, then tap the opposite that fits it</p>
    <div class="tile-bank">
      <button class="tile" data-tile="small">small</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="old">old</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="cold">cold</button>
      <button class="tile" data-tile="expensive">expensive</button>
    </div>
    <div class="slot-board">
      <button class="slot" data-slot="small" data-hint="Choose the size word that contrasts with big." data-fix="big ↔ small" data-placeholder="opposite of big">opposite of big</button>
      <button class="slot" data-slot="old" data-hint="Choose the age word that contrasts with new." data-fix="new ↔ old" data-placeholder="opposite of new">opposite of new</button>
      <button class="slot" data-slot="cold" data-hint="Choose the temperature word that contrasts with hot." data-fix="hot ↔ cold" data-placeholder="opposite of hot">opposite of hot</button>
      <button class="slot" data-slot="expensive" data-hint="Choose the price word that contrasts with cheap." data-fix="cheap ↔ expensive" data-placeholder="opposite of cheap">opposite of cheap</button>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-tiles>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-tiles>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose the quality word</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each adjective follows an explicit age, budget, temperature, size, or travel-time comparison.">
    <h3>Drill 2: choose the adjective the cue describes</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">The hint in brackets points to one clear answer</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="big">big</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="new">new</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hot">hot</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="expensive">expensive</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="fast">fast</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>I bought this phone yesterday, so it is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="new" data-hint="The purchase happened yesterday, not years ago." data-fix="I bought this phone yesterday, so it is new." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(not old)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>My watch budget is $100, but this watch costs $900, so it is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="expensive" data-hint="Compare the $900 price with the buyer's $100 budget." data-fix="For this buyer, the $900 watch is expensive." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(far above the budget)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The coffee just came out of the machine, so it is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hot" data-hint="Fresh coffee from the machine has a high temperature." data-fix="The coffee just came out of the machine, so it is hot." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(not cold)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Most offices nearby have five rooms. Our office has forty rooms, so it is very <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="big" data-hint="Compare forty rooms with the nearby offices that have five." data-fix="Compared with the nearby offices, our forty-room office is very big." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(larger than nearby offices)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>For the same route, the local train takes forty minutes and the express takes ten. The express is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="fast" data-hint="Compare ten minutes with forty minutes for the same route." data-fix="For the same route, the ten-minute express train is fast." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(not slow)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Word order: put the adjective in its home</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>For the combinations in this lesson, use <strong>size or quality + color + noun</strong>: <span class="ex">a small black notebook</span>, <span class="ex">a big red car</span>. Build these aloud after you finish each one.</p>

  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="a small black notebook" data-hint="Begin with a, place size before color, and finish with the noun." data-fix="a small black notebook" data-success-message="Correct. Before the noun, the A0 order is article + size + color + noun.">
    <h3>Drill 3: build the phrase</h3>
    <p class="tile-target">A notebook on your desk: small, and black. Build the phrase, then say it.</p>
